---
// Number Base Converter — convert a value between binary, octal, decimal,
// and hexadecimal live as you type, with an ASCII decode of the underlying
// bytes. Pure positional-notation arithmetic (src/utils/baseConvert.ts) —
// nothing here needs research or fact-checking, it's math. Same page shape
// as the other interactive tools (intro aside -> tool -> reference notes).
// IT-fundamentals content, not DFIR-specific — see consts.ts's IT_GROUPS
// comment and CLAUDE.md's "Nav grouping" invariant for the framing rule this
// page follows. Static page — prerender to HTML served from the CDN.
export const prerender = true;
import { css } from 'styled-system/css';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import BaseConverter from '../../components/BaseConverter.astro';
import { toolChip } from '../../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import ExternalResources from '../../components/ExternalResources.astro';
import { EXTERNAL_RESOURCES } from '../../data/externalResources';
import { NUMBER_BASES } from '../../utils/baseConvert';

const sectionH2 = css({
  display: 'flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  gap: '0.6rem',
  fontSize: '1.3rem',
  fontWeight: 700,
  mt: '2.5rem',
  mb: '1rem',
  pt: '1.5rem',
  borderTop: '1px solid token(colors.border)',
  scrollMarginTop: '90px',
});
const refTable = css({ width: '100%', borderCollapse: 'collapse', fontSize: '0.85rem' });
const refTh = css({ textAlign: 'left', color: 'textMuted', fontFamily: 'sans', fontWeight: 600, fontSize: '0.72rem', textTransform: 'uppercase', letterSpacing: '0.06em', p: '0.5rem 0.7rem', borderBottom: '1px solid token(colors.border)' });
const refTd = css({ p: '0.5rem 0.7rem', borderBottom: '1px solid token(colors.border)', color: 'text', verticalAlign: 'top' });
const refTdMuted = css({ p: '0.5rem 0.7rem', borderBottom: '1px solid token(colors.border)', color: 'textMuted', fontSize: '0.8rem', verticalAlign: 'top' });
const relatedList = css({ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', gap: '0.85rem' });
const relatedReason = css({ color: 'textMuted', fontSize: '0.85rem', mt: '0.35rem', lineHeight: 1.6 });
const inlineCode = css({ fontFamily: 'mono', fontSize: '0.92em', color: 'primary' });
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Number Base Converter"
  description="Convert a value between binary, octal, decimal, and hexadecimal live as you type, with an ASCII decode of the underlying bytes — entirely in your browser."
>
  <PageHeader
    breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Tool Catalog', href: '/tools/' }, { label: 'Number Base Converter' }]}
    eyebrow="Tools"
    title="Number Base Converter"
    description="Type a value into any base — binary, octal, decimal, or hexadecimal — and the other three update live, alongside an ASCII decode of the value's underlying bytes."
  />

  <aside
    class={css({
      bg: 'bgSubtle',
      border: '1px solid token(colors.border)',
      borderLeftWidth: '3px',
      borderLeftColor: 'primary',
      borderRadius: 'md',
      p: '1rem 1.25rem',
      mb: '1.5rem',
    })}
  >
    <p class={css({ color: 'text', lineHeight: 1.7, fontSize: '0.95rem', m: 0 })}>
      This is plain positional-notation arithmetic, computed with an arbitrary-precision integer type internally
      rather than a regular JavaScript number — so a value larger than the normal safe-integer ceiling still
      converts exactly, with no silent rounding. Nothing here is looked up or fact-checked, it's math, and it
      all happens locally, so nothing you type ever leaves your machine.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <p class={css({ color: 'text', lineHeight: 1.8, fontSize: '1rem', mb: '1.5rem' })}>
    Binary, octal, decimal, and hexadecimal are just four different ways of writing the same number, and
    switching between them by hand gets tedious fast — especially binary and hex, which is why they show up
    everywhere in computing in the first place (a byte is exactly two hex digits, and hex is a lot easier to
    read than a string of ones and zeros). This converter keeps all four in sync as you type into any one of
    them, and decodes the value's raw bytes as ASCII alongside it, so you can see at a glance whether a number
    is really just encoded text. That same skill — reading hex and binary fluently — comes up well beyond
    general IT work too: it's exactly what you're doing when you're eyeballing a hex dump from a memory image or
    picking a byte value out of raw packet capture.
  </p>

  <h2 id="calculator" class={css({ display: 'flex', alignItems: 'center', gap: '0.6rem', fontSize: '1.3rem', fontWeight: 700, mb: '1rem', scrollMarginTop: '90px' })}>
    <Icon name="hash" size={20} class={css({ color: 'primary' })} /> Calculator
  </h2>
  <BaseConverter />

  <h2 id="reference" class={sectionH2}>
    <Icon name="list" size={20} class={css({ color: 'primary' })} /> Base reference
  </h2>
  <div class={css({ overflowX: 'auto' })}>
    <table class={refTable}>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class={refTh}>Base</th>
          <th class={refTh}>Radix</th>
          <th class={refTh}>Digits</th>
          <th class={refTh}>Prefix</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          NUMBER_BASES.map((b) => (
            <tr>
              <td class={refTd}>
                <strong class={css({ color: 'text' })}>{b.label}</strong>
              </td>
              <td class={refTd}>{b.radix}</td>
              <td class={refTdMuted}>{b.digitRange}</td>
              <td class={refTdMuted}>{b.prefix ? <code class={inlineCode}>{b.prefix}</code> : '— (none)'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class={css({ color: 'textMuted', fontSize: '0.88rem', mt: '1.5rem', lineHeight: 1.7 })}>
    The ASCII decode reads the value's bytes from its hexadecimal form — every two hex digits is one byte — and
    checks each one against the printable ASCII range: <code class={inlineCode}>0x20</code> (space) through{' '}
    <code class={inlineCode}>0x7E</code> (<code class={inlineCode}>~</code>). A byte outside that range — a
    control character, or anything above <code class={inlineCode}>0x7E</code>, which covers all of
    extended/non-ASCII — means the value isn't really encoded text, at least not as plain ASCII, and the tool
    says so explicitly instead of rendering it as garbled characters.
  </p>

  <h2 id="related" class={sectionH2}>
    <Icon name="wrench" size={20} class={css({ color: 'primary' })} /> Related tools
  </h2>
  <div class={relatedList}>
    <div>
      <a href="/tools/hash-calculator/" class={toolChip}>
        <Icon name="lock" size={13} /> Hash Calculator <Icon name="arrow-right" size={13} />
      </a>
      <p class={relatedReason}>
        A computed hash is itself just a very large hex number — paste one in here to see its decimal or binary
        form.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <a href="/tools/cidr-calculator/" class={toolChip}>
        <Icon name="globe" size={13} /> CIDR &amp; VLAN Calculator <Icon name="arrow-right" size={13} />
      </a>
      <p class={relatedReason}>
        An IPv4 address is a 32-bit number under the hood, and subnet/netmask math is bitwise arithmetic on
        exactly that number — this tool converts the number, that one does the networking math with it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <ExternalResources resources={EXTERNAL_RESOURCES['base-converter']} />
</BaseLayout>
